---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Aside from '../../../components/Aside.astro';
import {funktorSidebar} from '../../../data/funktor-sidebar';
import {Code} from 'astro:components';
import {githubRepo} from '../../../data/site';
---

<DocsLayout title="Auth Module" sidebar={funktorSidebar}
            description="Multi-realm authentication with pluggable providers">

    <h1>Auth</h1>

    <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 !mt-2 !mb-8">
        Multi-realm authentication with email/password, Google SSO, GitHub SSO, and JWT tokens.
    </p>

    <h2>Realms</h2>

    <p>An auth realm represents a user population with its own providers, password policy, and user model.
        An app can have multiple realms &mdash; one for admin users, another for customers.</p>

    <Code code={`class AdminUserRealm(
    override val deps: AuthSystem.Deps,
    private val usersRepo: AdminUsersRepo,
) : AuthRealm<AdminUser> {

    override val id = RealmId("admin-user")

    override val providers = listOf(
        EmailAndPasswordAuth(deps),
        GoogleSsoAuth(deps, clientId = "your-google-client-id"),
        GithubSsoAuth(deps, clientId = "your-github-id", clientSecret = "your-github-secret"),
    )

    override val passwordPolicy = PasswordPolicy.default

    // Operations on users live on AuthUserAdapter, reached via \`realm.users\`.
    override val users = object : AuthUserAdapter<AdminUser> {
        override suspend fun loadById(id: UserId) = usersRepo.findById(id.value)

        // EmailAddress is always canonical (trimmed + lowercased), so this lookup is
        // case-insensitive by TYPE — callers no longer have to remember to normalize.
        override suspend fun loadByEmail(email: EmailAddress) = usersRepo.findByEmail(email)

        override suspend fun createForSignup(
            params: AuthUserAdapter.CreateUserForSignupParams,
        ): Stored<AdminUser> = usersRepo.insert(
            AdminUser(email = params.email, name = params.displayName),
        )

        // The name chosen on the activation page, written once the activation has won.
        override suspend fun activate(user: Stored<AdminUser>, params: AuthUserAdapter.ActivateUserParams) =
            usersRepo.save(user.modify { it.copy(name = params.displayName) })

        // A never-activated account gives up its address: rename every unique key,
        // and drop what was derived from it (the default name is its local part).
        override suspend fun retire(user: Stored<AdminUser>, retirement: AccountRetirement) =
            usersRepo.save(user.modify { it.copy(email = retirement.rename(it.email), name = "retired") })

        override suspend fun serialize(user: Stored<AdminUser>) = /* ... */
    }
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p><code>activate</code> and <code>retire</code> have no default. A realm without sign-up throws
        <code>AuthError.notSupported()</code> from both, as from <code>createForSignup</code>.</p>

    <h2>JWT generation</h2>

    <p>Each realm generates JWT tokens for authenticated users. The token includes user data
        and permissions, signed with HMAC-512.</p>

    <Code code={`override suspend fun generateJwt(
    user: Stored<AdminUser>,
    selectedOrg: SelectedOrg?,
): String {
    val gen = deps.jwtGenerator
    val userValue = user.resolve()

    val token = gen.createJwt(
        user = JwtUserData(
            id = UserId(user._id),
            desc = userValue.name,
            type = AdminUser.USER_TYPE,
            email = userValue.email,
        ),
        permissions = UserPermissions(isSuperUser = userValue.isSuperUser),
    ) {
        withExpiresAt(Kronos.systemUtc.instantNow().plus(1.hours).jvm)
    }

    return token
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p><code>selectedOrg</code> is <code>null</code> for an org-less realm. <code>JwtUserData.id</code> is a
        <code>UserId</code>, not a string &mdash; see <a href="#typed-identifiers">typed identifiers</a>.</p>

    <h2>Providers</h2>

    <p>Each provider handles a specific authentication mechanism:</p>

    <div class="not-prose grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8">
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/[0.02]">
            <h3 class="text-white font-semibold mb-1">EmailAndPasswordAuth</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">Sign up, sign in, password recovery, password change. Passwords are hashed
                with Argon2/Bcrypt and stored separately from user records.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/[0.02]">
            <h3 class="text-white font-semibold mb-1">GoogleSsoAuth</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">Google OAuth flow. Verifies ID tokens and creates or matches users by
                email.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/[0.02]">
            <h3 class="text-white font-semibold mb-1">GithubSsoAuth</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">GitHub OAuth flow. Exchanges authorization codes for access tokens. The
                identity is the public profile address, which GitHub must have verified: the app needs the
                <code>user:email</code> scope (a GitHub App: the "Email addresses" permission).</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="sign-up-and-activation">Sign-up and activation</h2>

    <p>A new account proves its email address before it holds anything. Sign-up takes the address only &mdash;
        no password, no name &mdash; mails a link and issues <strong>no session</strong>. The link opens
        &ldquo;Choose your password&rdquo;, which asks for the password and the display name; submitting it
        activates the account and signs the user in. Until then nothing can sign in to the account, and whoever
        signs up someone else's address has chosen nothing that its owner will later find in it.</p>

    <Code code={`// Sign-up: the address, nothing else (\`api\` is the realm's AuthApiClient)
api.signUp(AuthSignUpRequest.EmailAndPassword(provider = "email-password", email = email))

// The mailed link's page: the first password and the name activate the account
val response = authState.activateAccount(
    AuthActivateAccountRequest(provider, token, password = password, displayName = name),
)

val problem = when {
    response == null -> "The server could not be reached."
    response.success -> null // signed in: response.signIn
    response.failure == Failure.WeakPassword -> "Please choose a stronger password."
    response.failure == Failure.InvalidDisplayName -> AuthDisplayName.refusalOf(name)?.message
    else -> "This link is used, unknown or expired."
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>The password policy and the name are checked <em>before</em> the token is looked at. A refused input is
        a 200 with <code>success = false</code> and a <code>failure</code>; the link stays usable and the answer
        says nothing about it. A dead link gets <code>success = false</code> and no reason. On success,
        <code>signIn</code> is <code>Success</code>, <code>OrgSelectionRequired</code>, or <code>null</code>
        (no org yet, or a provider without sign-in). In an org realm, <code>AuthState</code> hands an
        <code>OrgSelectionRequired</code> to the login page, which opens on the org picker, once
        (<code>takeActivationOrgSelection()</code>).</p>

    <h3>The life of a pending account</h3>

    <div class="not-prose my-8">
        <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
            <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                <th class="py-2 pr-4 text-white font-semibold">When</th>
                <th class="py-2 pr-4 text-white font-semibold">What happens</th>
            </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-gray-400">
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4">The link is opened</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">Nothing. Only the page's POST activates, so a mail scanner that follows links activates nothing.</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4">The address signs up again</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">The mail is re-sent once the account's next delay has passed, and the answer is a fresh sign-up's, mail or no mail. Older links keep working for <code>activationLinkOverlap</code>, then die.</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4">&ldquo;Didn't sign up? Cancel&rdquo;</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">Every mail carries its own cancel link, valid as long as the account. It retires the account. The activation page's cancel button does the same with the activation link.</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4">A password reset completes</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">It proves the mailbox too, so it activates the account.</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4">SSO with the address</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">Activates the account if the provider verified the address (Google's <code>email_verified</code>, GitHub's verified public address), and deletes any earlier password; otherwise refused. The account has no password until a reset.</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Someone signs in</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">&ldquo;Invalid credentials&rdquo;, like a wrong password, with no hint to activate.</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>pendingAccountLifetime</code> passes</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">The account is retired at its next use (sign-up, activation, reset, SSO), or by <code>funktor/all</code>'s <code>PendingAccountsExpiryWorker</code>, which every app wired through <code>funktor(...)</code> runs every 15 minutes. The address is free again.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <p>Activation, cancel, retire, SSO and a reset that activates remove all of the sign-up's links. An account
        activated by reset or SSO is never asked for a name and keeps the default one, the address's local
        part.</p>

    <Aside>A pending address and a new one look the same to a caller. An active one does not: signing it up
        answers &ldquo;User already exists&rdquo;. So the sign-up form still tells whether an address has an
        account &mdash; only no longer whether that account is finished.
    </Aside>

    <p>&ldquo;Not activated&rdquo; is the <em>presence</em> of a marker record, not a flag on the user and not
        &ldquo;an unexpired token exists&rdquo;. A flag would need a default, and the only safe default locks out
        every existing account; the token test erases itself once the token expires.</p>

    <h3>Configuration</h3>

    <p>Per realm, in <code>RealmTokenConfig</code>:</p>

    <div class="not-prose my-8">
        <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
            <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                <th class="py-2 pr-4 text-white font-semibold">Setting</th>
                <th class="py-2 pr-4 text-white font-semibold">Default</th>
                <th class="py-2 pr-4 text-white font-semibold">Meaning</th>
            </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-gray-400">
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>pendingAccountLifetime</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">7 days</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">How long a never-activated account lives; no link outlives it.</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>emailVerificationTokenLifetime</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">24 h</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">How long an activation link works. It chooses the password and signs in, so it is a credential: shorten it where mail is forwarded or scanned.</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>activationResendDelays</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">5 min, 15 min, 1 h, 4 h, 12 h, 24 h</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">The wait before each re-sent mail, per account; the last entry repeats. It bounds the mail anyone who knows the address can cause.</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>activationLinkOverlap</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">30 min</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">How long the older links stay usable after a re-send, so a re-send cannot kill the link a user is typing into. 0: only the newest lives.</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>maxSignUpCancelTokens</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">12</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">Cancel links kept per account; past it the oldest goes. The default delays fit the sign-up mail and 11 re-sends into 7 days, so every mailed link works until the account ends. At least 2.</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>randomTokenByteLength</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">256</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">Random bytes per one-shot token. At least 32.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Retirement</h3>

    <p>Expiry and cancel <em>retire</em> the account: the framework deletes its auth records, calls
        <code>AuthUserAdapter.retire(user, AccountRetirement)</code>, and checks that the address no longer
        resolves to the account. The row stays, since other collections may point at it; its email becomes
        <code>retired-&lt;64 hex&gt;@&lt;32 hex&gt;#retired.invalid</code>, which keeps a SHA-256 of the address
        but not the address. <code>AccountRetirement.retiredKeyPrefix(email)</code> finds an address's retired
        rows, <code>AccountRetirement.isRetired(email)</code> tells them apart. A <code>retire()</code> that
        throws or renames nothing leaves the account pending, logs an error, and the next sweep tries again.
        <code>AuthRealm.DefaultMessaging</code> mails nothing to a retired address.</p>

    <h3>The display name</h3>

    <p><code>AuthDisplayName</code> lives in common code, so the activation page and the server apply the same
        rule: 1 to 100 UTF-16 chars after trimming, on one line, built of characters that show. Control,
        invisible and undisplayable characters are refused, as is a leading combining mark.
        <code>refusalOf(name)</code> says why, naming the character and where it sits:</p>

    <Code code={`AuthDisplayName.refusalOf(Char(0x200B) + "Ada")?.message
// Your name contains an invisible character (U+200B) at the start. It often comes along
// with copy and paste - please delete it, or type your name in again.

AuthDisplayName.normalizeOrNull("  Ada Lovelace ") // "Ada Lovelace"`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>An SSO sign-up keeps the provider's profile name when the rule accepts it, and otherwise uses the
        address's local part (lower-case, as the address is canonical).</p>

    <Aside>The flags of England, Scotland and Wales are refused. They are built from tag characters, the same
        ones that smuggle invisible text into a name, and the rule does not try to tell the two apart. Apologies
        to three nations.
    </Aside>

    <h3>One-shot tokens are stored hashed</h3>

    <p>Activation, cancel, reset, email-change and org-selection tokens are stored as their SHA-256
        (<code>TokenHash</code>); the raw token exists only in the mail or the answer. Whoever reads the auth
        records &mdash; a backup, a leak, an admin tool &mdash; finds no working link. The hash is unsalted and
        fast, which is safe only because the token is long and random: hence the 32-byte floor.</p>

    <p>SSO reaches an account only through an address the provider verified, for an active account, a pending
        one and a new one alike. Otherwise anyone with a provider account that claims an address could sign in
        to its owner's account, or create it before them.</p>

    <h2>Organisations</h2>

    <p>A realm declares how it relates to organisations through its <code>orgPolicy</code>. The default is
        <code>OrgPolicy.None</code> &mdash; an org-less realm, such as an internal operator console.</p>

    <p>Under <code>OrgPolicy.Required</code>, sign-in resolves the user's accessible organisations and
        answers with the 0/1/n flow: none is a refusal, exactly one auto-selects, and several return an
        <code>OrgSelectionRequired</code> response backed by a single-use selection token. Refresh keeps the
        same organisation &mdash; it never silently changes which one is active.</p>

    <Code code={`class B2bRealm(...) : AuthRealm<B2bUser> {
    override val orgPolicy = OrgPolicy.Required()

    override suspend fun getMemberships(user: Stored<B2bUser>): Set<OrgMembership> =
        orgMembers.sessionMembershipsOf(UserId(user._id))

    // How "accessible" is defined is the APP's call — the framework only asks.
    // The demo filters to active orgs and dedupes; see saas_org_hooks.kt.
    override suspend fun getAccessibleOrgs(memberships: Set<OrgMembership>): List<AuthOrgRef> =
        orgs.accessibleActiveOrgs(memberships)
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <h2 id="typed-identifiers">Typed identifiers</h2>

    <p>Identity values are value classes rather than raw strings &mdash; <code>RealmId</code>,
        <code>UserId</code>, <code>OrgId</code> and <code>EmailAddress</code>. They are threaded end to end:
        through JWT claims, URI parameters, storage and the API surface.</p>

    <p><code>EmailAddress</code> canonicalizes on construction (trim + lower-case), which is what makes
        lookups case-insensitive by type rather than by remembering to call <code>.lowercase()</code> at
        every call site.</p>

    <h2>Localized emails</h2>

    <p>Auth emails render per locale. The language is chosen from the user's own setting, then the realm's
        <code>defaultLanguage</code>, then the framework's guaranteed fallback &mdash; a chain, not an
        either/or, so a German-only deployment does not mail English to a user whose stored language it has
        no rendering for.</p>

    <Code code={`class MyRealm(...) : AuthRealm<MyUser> {
    override val defaultLanguage = Locale("de")

    override val messaging = AuthRealm.DefaultMessaging(
        senderEmail = "noreply@example.com",
        senderName = "Example",
        applicationName = "Example",
        realm = this,
        templates = AuthEmailTemplates.default(myBrandedLayout),
    )
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>See <a href="/ultra/funktor/messaging">Messaging</a> for the template and layout surface.</p>

    <h2>Wiring in Kontainer</h2>

    <p>Register your realm and its dependencies as a Kontainer module:</p>

    <Code code={`val AdminUserModule = module {
    dynamic(AdminUsersRepo::class)
    dynamic(AdminUserRealm::class)
    dynamic(AdminUserServices::class)
}

// In your main blueprint:
val blueprint = kontainer {
    funktor(
        config = config,
        auth = { useKarango() },
        // ...
    )

    module(AdminUserModule)
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <h2>Auth API</h2>

    <p>The auth module provides pre-built API endpoints for all auth operations:
        sign up, sign in, org selection, activate account, cancel sign-up
        (<code>POST /auth/{'{realm}'}/activate/cancel</code>), set password, password recovery, token refresh,
        and user-specific API access queries.
        A matching client library (<code>AuthApiClient</code>) is available for Kotlin/JS frontends.</p>

    <h2>Token refresh</h2>

    <p>The <code>GET /auth/{'{realm}'}/refresh-token</code> endpoint issues a fresh JWT for authenticated users.
        The server re-loads the user and regenerates the token, so permission changes are picked up automatically.</p>

    <Code code={`// Frontend: the token is refreshed automatically by AuthState.
// You can also call it manually:
val response = api.auth.refreshToken().firstOrNull()
// response.data?.token  — the new JWT`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <h2>API access matrix (ApiAcl)</h2>

    <p>The <code>GET /auth/my-api-access</code> endpoint returns a flat list of API endpoints the current user
        can access, with their estimated access level (<code>Granted</code> or <code>Partial</code>).
        Denied endpoints are filtered out &mdash; the response only reveals what the user CAN access.</p>

    <p><strong>Advisory only.</strong> <code>ApiAcl</code> decides what to RENDER. The server remains the
        authority and nothing here enforces anything &mdash; never use it in place of an auth rule.</p>

    <Code code={`// Fetch the access matrix once after login
val matrix = api.auth.getMyApiAccess().first().data!!
val acl = ApiAcl(matrix)

// Type-safe lookup using the same endpoint objects from your ApiClient
if (acl.canAccess(MyApiClient.GetEvent)) {
    navLink("/events") { +"Events" }
}

// Destructive actions too — Partial IS access; the backend checks ownership
if (acl.canAccess(MyApiClient.DeleteEvent)) {
    button { +"Delete" }
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>Four predicates, sharing their names with the generated TypeScript SDK so both clients read the
        same way. <code>canAccess</code> is true for anything but <code>Denied</code> &mdash; it
        <strong>includes <code>Partial</code>, and that is access</strong>: <code>Partial</code> means
        the route is callable and the server will additionally check the arguments &mdash; typically
        "do you own this resource". So <code>canAccess</code> is the predicate for visibility,
        destructive actions included; gating a delete on <code>canFullyAccess</code> would hide it
        from the user on their own resource. <code>canPartiallyAccess</code> is <code>Partial</code>
        only and <code>isDenied</code> is the negation of <code>canAccess</code>. Only a rule that
        <em>estimates</em> <code>Partial</code> produces it &mdash; <code>forCall</code> given an
        <code>estimateFn</code>, or an <code>AccessLevelCheck</code>; the built-in rules never do, so do
        not gate on <code>canPartiallyAccess</code> expecting the branch to run.</p>

    <p><code>canFullyAccess</code> means only that no auth rule <em>reported</em> an argument check
        &mdash; <strong>not</strong> that the route works for every resource. A <code>forCall</code>
        rule estimates <code>Granted</code> unless it is given an <code>estimateFn</code>, so an
        ownership check written with it reads as full access; checks inside a handler, and
        <code>RouteParamsGuard</code>s such as the saas <code>OrgIsolationGuard</code>, are not auth
        rules and are invisible too.</p>

    <p><code>ApiAcl.empty</code> denies everything, and unknown endpoints default to
        <code>Denied</code>. Note that this includes <code>public()</code> routes &mdash; a logged-out
        visitor has no matrix at all, so do not gate a sign-in control on the ACL.</p>

    <h2>Frontend support</h2>

    <p>Pre-built <a href="/ultra/kraft/">Kraft</a> components for login pages, password reset, and password change
        widgets.
        Auth state management with router middleware that redirects unauthenticated users to the login page.</p>

    <h3>Session lifecycle</h3>

    <p><code>AuthState</code> automatically manages the JWT session lifecycle. Enabled by default with zero
        configuration:</p>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>Periodic refresh</strong> &mdash; checks token expiry every 60 seconds, refreshes 2 minutes before
            it expires
        </li>
        <li><strong>Focus check</strong> &mdash; verifies token when the browser tab regains focus</li>
        <li><strong>Graceful expiry</strong> &mdash; on expiry: saves the current page, logs out, redirects to login.
            After re-login, navigates back to the previous page
        </li>
        <li><strong>Page reload</strong> &mdash; clears expired tokens from localStorage immediately on app init</li>
    </ul>

    <Code code={`// Zero-config: auto-refresh is enabled by default
val auth = authState<MyUser>(
    frontend = AuthFrontend.default(config = AuthFrontendConfig(redirectAfterLogin = Nav.dashboard())),
    api = Apis.auth,
    router = { kraft.router },
)

// With custom config:
val auth = authState<MyUser>(
    frontend = AuthFrontend.default(config = AuthFrontendConfig(redirectAfterLogin = Nav.dashboard())),
    api = Apis.auth,
    router = { kraft.router },
    sessionConfig = AuthSessionConfig(
        checkIntervalMs = 30_000,          // check every 30 seconds
        refreshBeforeExpiryMs = 120_000L,  // refresh 2 minutes before expiry
        onTokenRefreshed = { refreshApiAcl() },
        onSessionExpired = { showLoginDialog() },
    ),
)

// Disable auto-refresh entirely:
sessionConfig = AuthSessionConfig.disabled`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <Aside>The auth module handles the plumbing &mdash; token storage, JWT generation, password hashing,
        recovery tokens, session lifecycle. You implement the user model and the realm, including how an
        account gives up its address. Only your repository knows which of its fields are unique.
    </Aside>

    <h2>Storage backends</h2>

    <p>Auth records (passwords, one-shot tokens, sessions) are stored via pluggable backends:</p>

    <ul>
        <li><code>useKarango()</code> &mdash; ArangoDB via <a href="/ultra/karango/">Karango</a></li>
        <li><code>useMonko()</code> &mdash; MongoDB via <a href="/ultra/monko/">Monko</a></li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="upgrading">Upgrading to email-first sign-up</h2>

    <p>The compiler walks you through most of the change; the <a href={`${githubRepo}/blob/master/CHANGELOG.md`}
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">changelog</a> lists the API. What follows gives no compile signal.</p>

    <p><strong>An app with its own pages, mails or routes:</strong></p>

    <ul>
        <li>a sign-up form that still sends a password or a name: the server ignores both;</li>
        <li>an activation page must POST <code>password</code> and <code>displayName</code> (without the name:
            a 400, and the link stays usable), and must never POST on load, or mail scanners activate accounts;</li>
        <li>an activation mail template must render <code>cancelUrl</code>, and its copy should say the link
            chooses the password;</li>
        <li>a router of its own must mount <code>AuthFrontendRoutes.cancelSignUp</code>
            (<code>AuthState.mount</code> does).</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>An app with its own providers or hooks:</strong></p>

    <ul>
        <li>an SSO provider must route every existing account through <code>AuthRealm.resolveSsoAccount</code>,
            which now asks <code>isEmailVerified</code> for active accounts too, and must check verification
            itself before <code>createForSignup</code>;</li>
        <li>a name it passes to <code>CreateUserForSignupParams.of</code> that the rule refuses becomes the
            local part;</li>
        <li>an <code>AuthRealm.Messaging</code> of its own lacks <code>DefaultMessaging</code>'s guard against
            mailing a retired address;</li>
        <li><code>AuthRecordStorage.OnAfterSave</code> listeners do not see the conditional writes (claims,
            re-send schedules, activations).</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Every app:</strong></p>

    <ul>
        <li>links mailed before tokens were hashed stop working. Old one-shot rows (<code>token</code>, no
            <code>tokenHash</code>) cannot be read back; delete them from dev databases. Rows of type
            <code>activation-resend-token</code> are dead weight too;</li>
        <li>accounts left pending from before email first still hold their sign-up password. It no longer signs
            in; the activation link replaces it, and SSO deletes it;</li>
        <li>the first expiry sweep retires every pending account older than <code>pendingAccountLifetime</code>,
            and retired rows stay in the user collection, where lists and admin screens show them unless they filter
            <code>AccountRetirement.isRetired</code>;</li>
        <li>without <code>funktor/all</code>'s <code>Funktor</code> module there is no sweep: an expired account
            is retired only when it is used again;</li>
        <li>GitHub sign-in needs the <code>user:email</code> scope (a GitHub App: the &ldquo;Email
            addresses&rdquo; permission). <code>GithubSignInButton</code> asks for it, sends a one-time
            <code>state</code> and returns to the page's route, also under hash routing; a GitHub authorize URL the
            app builds itself is no longer acted on;</li>
        <li>a Google account with an unverified address can no longer sign in, also to an account SSO created
            earlier. Sessions taken that way before the upgrade survive it; rotating the JWT signing key ends
            them;</li>
        <li><code>RealmTokenConfig</code> refuses <code>randomTokenByteLength</code> below 32 and
            <code>maxSignUpCancelTokens</code> below 2, at construction.</li>
    </ul>

